   1   2.5  ~~
          Socrates depicts chained cave-prisoners who can perceive only shadows and echoes,
          establishing them as like ourselves in enlightenment.
   2 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows that release from illusion begins with painful glare, confusion, and
          resistance, as the prisoner clings to familiar shadows before being dazzled by greater
          realities up to the sun.
   3 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates traces the freed prisoner's gradual ascent from shadows and reflections to
          the sun itself as cause of the visible world, leaving him to pity his former den and
          despise its shadow-honours.
   4 47.25  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows the returning philosopher blinded, ridiculed, and threatened with
          death, then explicitly decodes the cave as the visible world and crowns the ascent
          with the Idea of the Good.
   5  30.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates excuses the enlightened philosopher's unwillingness to return and ridiculous
          blindness in human affairs, then teaches that dazzlement comes from either entering
          light or darkness and should be diagnosed rather than mocked.
   6  20.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates rejects teachers who claim to insert knowledge into the soul, arguing that
          education is instead the art of turning the soul's innate power from becoming toward
          being and the Good.
   7  18.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates argues that keen natures freed from sensual drag could see truth sharply, so
          the founders must compel those who reach the Good to descend again and govern.
   8  20.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates answers Glaucon's charge of injustice by arguing that educated philosophers
          owe ruling service to the whole State, whose reluctant rulers will govern best.
   9  19.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates concludes that just philosophic pupils will accept ruling as a stern
          necessity, and only rulers given a better life of philosophy and rich in virtue can
          keep the State well-ordered.

