Chained people who take shadows for truth climb painfully to sunlight, return to scorn, and learn that schooling turns the soul so the enlightened must reluctantly go back to govern wisely.

1. Shadows Mistaken For Reality  (passages 1-3)
    People chained since childhood take shadows and echoes for truth and suffer pain and
    confusion when dragged toward light and at last the sun.
       -> The painful climb to sunlight raises why anyone would return and what the whole story
       really means.
2. Return Blinded And Explained  (passages 4-5)
    The freed person stumbles blind back underground to laughter and danger, while the cave is
    explained as our visible world crowned by supreme goodness.
       -> Learning to tell dazzlement from entering light or darkness opens a new idea of
       schooling as turning rather than filling.
3. Turning Souls Toward Light  (passages 6-7)
    Learning is revealed not as putting sight into blind eyes but as turning an inborn power
    away from lower cravings toward reality.
       -> If schooling turns souls upward, the city must force those who see clearly to go back
       down and lead.
4. Forced To Govern Justly  (passages 8-9)
    Thinkers raised in wisdom must accept ruling as an unwelcome duty, bringing calm order
    through love of wisdom over ambition.
